Sign in once in a setup project and save the session, then let every spec start already signed in.

```ts
// auth.setup.ts
import { test as setup, expect } from '@playwright/test';

setup('sign in once', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.E2E_USER!);
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.E2E_PASSWORD!);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
  await page.context().storageState({ path: '.auth/user.json' });
});
```

```ts
// invites.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('a new invite appears in the table', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/invites');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Invite user' }).click();
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('new.person@example.com');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Send invite' }).click();

  const row = page.getByRole('row', { name: /new\.person@example\.com/ });
  await expect(row).toBeVisible();
  await expect(row.getByText('Pending')).toBeVisible();
});
```

In `playwright.config.ts`, give the setup its own project and have the test project depend on it with `storageState: '.auth/user.json'`. Add `projects: [{ name: 'chromium', use: devices['Desktop Chrome'] }]` so CI pulls one browser.

The two credentials come from the environment, so nothing secret is committed. Add `.auth/` to `.gitignore`: the saved session is a live credential.

